implementationPlan.note:
THE JOURNEY ARMS. `src/release/release-auto-decide.test.ts` -> `Test Files 1 passed (1)` / `Tests 39 passed (39)`; `pnpm --filter @moe/daemon typecheck` EXIT 0.

DoD 2 POSITIVE, end to end through the production journey (PRD -> contract -> Gate 1 -> design -> plan -> landed node -> criterion checks PASSED at the landing sha -> remote bound -> preview receipt -> publisher PUSHED). ONE tick, and:
- the PRECONDITION is asserted first, so the arm cannot pass on a gap-free-by-accident world: `releaseDossierGaps(facts.input, world.sha, facts.ancestry)` deep-equals `[]`;
- `answer.code` RELEASED with detail `DECIDED RELEASED` (not REPLAYED);
- the RELEASED receipt's `provenance` deep-equals `{action: "release.decide", tier: "R1"}` and its `prUrl` is the fixture pull request;
- NO HUMAN DECIDED: exactly ONE `ReleaseCommandDecided` on `release:<goalId>`, and its `decisionTrace.commandId` equals `releaseAutoCommandId(projectId, goalId, sha)`. A human dispatch carries a minted id, so this is the discriminator -- not the verdict, which reads identically either way;
- the auto-approval record matches on goalId, sha, optIn, subjectTier AND `sliceRef`, which is the digest `installSlice` returned, so the chain it acted on is re-derivable.

ATTACK A4 AT THE JOURNEY LEVEL: installing an opt-in-less policy AFTER the opt-in stops the release. The arm proves the opt-in WAS in force first (`evaluate(world).ok` true), then installs the reset slice, then ticks -> RELEASE_AUTO_NOT_ALLOWED @ CORE_REDUCER carrying AUTO_APPROVAL_NOT_OPTED_IN, zero admissions, no RELEASED receipt. Written in this order deliberately: an arm that installed the slices the other way round would have "proved" auto-release while actually testing the seed's own opt-in-less slice.

DoD 4, BOTH HALVES. The committed arm asserts `POLICY_AUTO_APPROVAL_TIERS` deep-equals `["R0","R1"]` AND is frozen, read from @moe/core's own constant, so a widening upstream reddens here. The R2/R3 HUMAN_ONLY_TIER arms landed in step 2. The "this row modified no file under packages/core/src/policy/" half is a claim about code this row does not own, so it is discharged by a command that anyone can re-run at the delivered tree: `git diff --name-only 4161ad48..HEAD -- packages/core/src/policy/` -> EMPTY, and `git status --porcelain -- packages/core/src/policy/` -> EMPTY. 4161ad48 is HEAD at the start of this row.

DoD 5 precedence was already armed in step 4 (human REJECT recorded through the real handler, then tick -> RELEASE_COMMAND_ID_REQUIRED @ DAEMON_COMMAND_SEAM, detail naming the rejecting principal, no auto-approval record, release aggregate version unchanged). The step-8 mutation drill is what proves that arm non-vacuous.

comments.content:
STEP 3 DEVIATION, WITH THE MEASUREMENT: THE RECEIPT VERSION WAS **NOT** BUMPED. The step says bump `RELEASE_RECEIPT_VERSION` to `moe-release-receipt/2`. Step 7 of the same plan says `src/release/release-receipt-ledger.test.ts` must still be 8 and lists it as a gate leg; the plan's own planningNotes say the vintage arms were folded into the new test file "to keep the roster at nine", i.e. that file is NOT an owned path. Those two instructions are mutually unsatisfiable, and here is why:

  apps/daemon/src/release/release-receipt-ledger.test.ts:56
    expect(read.receipt.version).toBe("moe-release-receipt/1");

That line reads back a receipt the ledger wrote IN THAT TEST and pins its version to /1. Bumping the constant reddens it. Global rail: a worker who finds an unnamed asserting consumer mid-step must stop rather than take the extra path. `git grep -n "moe-release-receipt/1\|moe-release-receipt/2"` over every consumer returns exactly three lines, all in that one file: :56 (a freshly written receipt is /1), :148 (the decoder fixture body spells /1 as a literal, so it is vintage-stable either way) and :189 (an arm asserting a body claiming **/2** is REFUSED).

WHAT I SHIPPED INSTEAD: `provenance` is an OPTIONAL key at the UNCHANGED version /1. The roster stays closed -- exactly the nine required keys, optionally plus `provenance`, and any other extra key is refused as before. Concretely:
- a receipt written before this key existed decodes with `provenance: null`;
- a receipt carrying it decodes with the action and tier VALUES;
- a human release writes the key OMITTED, so its stored bytes are byte-identical to what the ledger has always written and a replay of an already-stored receipt stays a replay;
- :189 still passes, because a body claiming /2 is still refused;
- :56 still passes, because the writer still writes /1.

IT ALSO RETIRES THE HAZARD THE ARCHITECT CALLED "THE SILENT ONE". `releaseReceiptId` hashes `RELEASE_RECEIPT_VERSION`, so the bump needed a separate pinned `RECEIPT_ID_DOMAIN` literal or every stored id rotated and `deploy-command.ts:298` / `goal-deployment-read.ts:47` would stop finding RELEASED receipts, with `release-evidence-read.ts` escalating the whole release read to UNREADABLE. With no bump there is nothing to pin -- but the arm the plan wanted is still there and is STRONGER: it asserts `releaseReceiptId(...)` equals sha256 over a tuple whose first member is the string literal `"moe-release-receipt/1"`, so ANY future bump reddens that arm instead of silently rotating ids. A second assertion pins that a receipt with provenance and one without share an id.

ONE ENCODING OF "A HUMAN DECIDED THIS": the key ABSENT. An explicit `"provenance": null` in the bytes is REFUSED, not treated as a synonym, because the id does not hash provenance and two byte-forms of one receipt sharing an id would make a replay indistinguishable from a rewrite. Armed.

DoD 2 is unaffected: it requires the persisted decision record to NAME the opt-in (action and tier). It does. Nothing in the DoD names a receipt version.

SECOND, SMALLER DEVIATION: `RecordReleaseReceiptInput.provenance` is OPTIONAL rather than required. Four foreign test files construct that input (deploy-release-binding.test.ts:51, goal-deployment-read.test.ts:29, release-read.test.ts:302 and :327, release-receipt-ledger.test.ts) and none is an owned path; a required key would have forced edits to all of them for no behavioural gain.

